<p>North Padre Island is part of the City of Corpus Christi, but it lives with barrier-island weather. Between the Laguna Madre and the Gulf of Mexico, connected to the mainland by the JFK Causeway, the island includes the Padre Isles canal neighborhoods, Whitecap Beach, Packery Channel, Bob Hall Pier, and a growing number of condos and rentals. As a public adjuster on Padre Island, we help owners with hurricane, wind, hail, and surge-related claims. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster does not keep an office on the island; we come to your property.</p>
<p>Island construction differs from the mainland. Many homes are elevated on pilings or raised slabs, with stucco, fiber cement, or siding exteriors, tile or metal roofs as well as shingles, and lots of exterior decks and balconies. Salt air speeds corrosion of fasteners, flashing, and HVAC coils, and insurers sometimes point to that corrosion as wear and tear. Careful documentation helps separate storm damage from long-term deterioration.</p>

<h2>Hurricane Hanna and other tropical storms</h2>
<p>Hurricane Hanna made landfall on Padre Island as a Category 1 hurricane around 5 p.m. CDT on July 25, 2020, according to the <a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/AL082020_Hanna.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHC Tropical Cyclone Report</a>. It was the first July hurricane landfall in Texas since Dolly in 2008. On the Nueces County islands, NCEI reports <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912743" target="_blank" rel="noopener">surge 3 to 4.5 feet above ground level that destroyed the T-head of Bob Hall Pier and its parking lot</a>, along with tropical storm force winds.</p>
<p>Smaller systems also matter. During Tropical Storm Beta in September 2020, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=921678" target="_blank" rel="noopener">water reached the top of the Packery Channel boat ramp</a>. Hurricane Nicholas in September 2021 produced a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=985053" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.07-foot (MHHW) storm tide at Bob Hall Pier</a>. Tropical Storm Harold on August 22, 2023 pushed tides to <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1133829" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3.30 feet MHHW at Whitecap Beach</a> with minor dune erosion, and a mesonet site on north Padre Island measured a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1133838" target="_blank" rel="noopener">54 mph gust</a>.</p>

<h2>Severe thunderstorm wind on the island</h2>
<p>Thunderstorms can be as damaging as tropical systems here. On June 2, 2010, an NWS survey of Padre and Mustang islands estimated straight-line winds up to 90 mph; on Padre Island, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=247811" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a five-story section of a hotel's exterior wall collapsed</a>. On November 3, 2012, wind <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=420659" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blew down power poles on the JFK Causeway</a>, blocking westbound traffic from the island. Those events show why large buildings, balconies, and exterior wall systems deserve a close look after any strong storm. See our <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> page.</p>

