<h2>Insurance on Padre Island: TWIA, NFIP, and CRS</h2>
<p>Nueces County is in the TWIA designated catastrophe area, and most island owners carry three policies: a homeowners or condo policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a flood policy. TWIA covers wind and hail only. Properties in V, VE, or V1-30 flood zones that were built, altered, remodeled, or enlarged on or after September 1, 2009 must show proof of NFIP flood coverage, when available, to get TWIA coverage.</p>
<p>Because the island is inside the City of Corpus Christi, the city's NFIP status applies. According to FEMA Community Status Book data (April 2025), Corpus Christi participates in the NFIP and the Community Rating System at Class 8. NFIP residential limits are $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents, and coverage generally begins 30 days after purchase. When wind and surge both hit, the split between TWIA and flood can decide the outcome of the claim; our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage</a> explains how that is usually analyzed.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of damage</th><th>Policy that usually responds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roof, windows, and siding damaged by wind or hail</td><td>TWIA windstorm policy (or the homeowners policy if it includes wind and hail)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain entering through a wind-created opening</td><td>Usually the policy covering wind, subject to its terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Surge or rising water in the lower enclosure or living area</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fire, theft, or plumbing leaks</td><td>Homeowners or condo policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Codes, permits, and windstorm certificates</h2>
<p>Island repairs follow City of Corpus Christi codes, adopted with local amendments effective August 1, 2023, and the city's re-roof permit submission includes a TDI WPI-1 form from the windstorm engineer or inspector. A city framing inspection does not replace TDI windstorm inspections or certifications. For TWIA eligibility, work done on or after January 1, 1988 needs a windstorm certificate of compliance. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">Corpus Christi building codes guide</a> covers these steps and how ordinance or law coverage may help with code-required costs.</p>

<h2>How we help island owners</h2>
<p>We inspect the roof, building envelope, balconies, decks, mechanical equipment, and interior, and we separate wind and water damage in our documentation where both occurred. We prepare an itemized estimate and communicate with TWIA, your flood insurer, and your homeowners carrier. Check live Packery Channel tide data on our <a href="/weather/">weather and tides page</a>, and see <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA claim help</a> and <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims</a> for more.</p>
<p>Many island homes are second homes or rentals. If you are away when a storm hits, ask someone you trust to photograph the property before cleanup, and report the claim promptly. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can inspect the property and keep you updated if you are out of town.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call or text [[phone]].</p>
